The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra states that every non-constant single-variable polynomial with complex coefficients has at least one complex root. This theorem has multiple well-known proofs, including approaches using topology, complex analysis (e.g., Liouville's theorem), and algebraic methods, some of which employ induction (e.g., induction on the degree after establishing properties of real polynomials or using splitting fields). It is most famously associated with Carl Friedrich Gauss, the towering German genius who provided four distinct proofs over his lifetime, beginning with his doctoral thesis in 1799.

Thus, the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra is the result in question.

\boxed{\text{Algebra}}

The description matches the Republic of Nauru. Nauru is a small island nation in the Pacific Ocean, with an area of just 21 square kilometers, making it "miniscule." It was annexed by Germany, a great continental power, in 1888 as part of German New Guinea. After World War I, it came under Australian administration but gained independence in 1968 as the Republic of Nauru. English is widely spoken (alongside Nauruan) and used in government and commerce, and the majority of the population is Christian (approximately two-thirds Protestant and one-third Catholic).

Thus, the answer is Nauru.

\boxed{\text{Nauru}}
